        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe move is mostly clean and idiomatic \u2014 `luhn` correctly relocates to the generation site (where the CHK is now produced), `memberIdRegion` is a well-factored private parser reused by both `formatLocality` and `formatFiscalYear` with a clear note on why it's private, and the rename is applied consistently across model, mapper, event, schema and OpenAPI. One structural issue, though:\n\n**The fiscal-year rule is now duplicated across two units.**\n\n- `OwnerMapper.fiscalYearEndYear` (`OwnerMapper.java:286-288`) encodes the July-cutoff rule: `month >= JULY ? year + 1 : year`. Its Javadoc presents it as *the* definition of the fiscal year's naming.\n- `ClinicServiceImpl.generateMemberId` (`ClinicServiceImpl.java:273-274`) re-encodes the identical rule inline: `(registrationDate.getMonthValue() >= 7 ? registrationDate.getYear() + 1 : registrationDate.getYear()) % 100`.\n\nThis is the same business rule written twice, in two places a maintainer has to keep in sync. And the drift this invites is already visible: the service applies `BusinessDays.effectiveRegistrationDate(...)` before deriving the year, while the mapper's `formatFiscalYear` fallback (`OwnerMapper.java:277`) feeds the raw `owner.getRegistrationDate()` straight into `fiscalYearEndYear` with no business-day adjustment \u2014 so the two \"fiscal year\" derivations can disagree for the same owner. Whatever the intended semantics, the rule should have one home.\n\nConcretely: extract the fiscal-year-end-year derivation into a single shared location \u2014 e.g. a static `fiscalYearEndYear(LocalDate)` (and/or a 2-digit variant) on `BusinessDays`, alongside the existing `effectiveRegistrationDate`, since that class already owns the registration-date policy. Then have both `generateMemberId` and the mapper call it, so the write path and the read-path fallback share one definition (including a single, deliberate decision about whether the business-day adjustment is part of it).\n\nMinor, optional: the memberId layout (`<REGION><FY2><HASH8><CHK>`) is now constructed in `generateMemberId` and taken apart in `memberIdRegion`/`formatFiscalYear` with the \"2 digits after the region\" offset hard-coded on the read side. That write/read split is inherent, but if you touch this again, a shared note or small parse helper describing the segment layout in one place would reduce the chance the two sides drift."
